https://satoshinotebook.com/trust-is-a-problem,h3,The Inevitability
https://satoshinotebook.com/trust-is-a-problem,p,"Throughout history, every monetary system has shared one fatal flaw: the requirement to trust someone — a ruler, a bank, a government — not to abuse their power over money."
https://satoshinotebook.com/trust-is-a-problem,p,"Money requires trust to function, yet this very trust inevitably leads to its corruption. When you hold any form of money, you're trusting the issuer not to debase it, the custodian not to seize it, and the system not to collapse."
https://satoshinotebook.com/trust-is-a-problem,p,This pattern of monetary betrayal repeats throughout history:
https://satoshinotebook.com/trust-is-a-problem,p,"Authorities face a constant choice: either tax their citizens directly or print more money. History shows they inevitably choose the latter. Why? As Saylor explains, ""It's a lot easier to print money than it is to tax people."""
https://satoshinotebook.com/trust-is-a-problem,p,Consider these manifestations:
https://satoshinotebook.com/trust-is-a-problem,p,"When money requires trust, it fails as property. History shows us countless examples:"
